WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Downloaded @ subs4free.club

﻿1

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:04.296
   <i> My name is Anthony Powell.</i>


00:01:04.298 --> 00:01:07.766
  <i> I grew up on this dairy farm</i>
   <i> in Taranaki, New Zealand.</i>


00:01:07.768 --> 00:01:09.435
  <i> But I've actually spent most</i>


00:01:09.437 --> 00:01:13.605
    <i> of my adult life living</i>
   <i> and working in Antarctica.</i>


00:01:13.607 --> 00:01:16.275
    <i> The most common question</i>
         <i> I get asked is</i>


00:01:16.277 --> 00:01:18.510
    <i> "What's it like to live</i>
          <i> down there?"</i>


00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:22.147
     <i> It's so hard to answer</i>
         <i> that in words</i>


00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:25.317
   <i> and it's never really been</i>
    <i> captured on film before.</i>


00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:29.488
  <i> But there's something about</i>
     <i> time lapse photography</i>


00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:33.425
      <i> that brings to life</i>
  <i> the dynamic forces of nature</i>


00:01:33.427 --> 00:01:35.828
       <i> that you can feel</i>
     <i> and sense around you,</i>


00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:38.297
  <i> but you can't actually see.</i>


00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:43.435
   <i> So I've taught myself how</i>
  <i> to use a variety of cameras</i>


00:01:43.437 --> 00:01:46.638
    <i> and built some homemade</i>
   <i> equipment in my spare time</i>


00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.641
    <i> that can still function</i>
      <i> in the extreme cold.</i>


00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:37.456
    <i> It's taken me more than</i>
  <i> 10 years to make this film.</i>


00:02:37.458 --> 00:02:39.291
         <i> I've worn out</i>
      <i> thousands of dollars</i>


00:02:39.293 --> 00:02:40.726
    <i> worth of camera gear...</i>


00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:45.831
   <i> and spent countless hours</i>
 <i> standing in the freezing cold</i>


00:02:45.833 --> 00:02:49.835
    <i> in an effort to capture</i>
 <i> the true feeling of this vast</i>


00:02:49.837 --> 00:02:51.203
        <i> important place.</i>


00:02:55.575 --> 00:02:58.877
<i> There really are only</i>
<i><i><i><i>   <i> two seasons in Antarctica:</i>


00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:01.947
    <i> a busy summer when most</i>
   <i> of the science happens...</i>


00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:06.885
  <i> and a wild and lonely winter</i>


00:03:06.887 --> 00:03:09.321
      <i> that few people will</i>
        <i> ever experience.</i>


00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:17.663
<i>To understand the place properly</i>


00:03:17.665 --> 00:03:20.432
    <i> you really need to spend</i>
         <i> one full year</i>


00:03:20.434 --> 00:03:22.501
     <i> down here on the ice.</i>


00:05:12.812 --> 00:05:14.646
    <i> I think that most people</i>


00:05:14.648 --> 00:05:17.015
<i>think that we're all scientists.</i>


00:05:17.017 --> 00:05:19.918
     <i> There are real people</i>
           <i> down here.</i>


00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.454
         <i> It's not just</i>
       <i> the people you see</i>


00:05:22.456 --> 00:05:24.423
   <i> on the National Geographic</i>
            <i> channels</i>


00:05:24.425 --> 00:05:27.392
       <i> or it's not people</i>
         <i> who can afford</i>


00:05:27.394 --> 00:05:29.461
       <i> a $10,000 cruise.</i>


00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:33.999
    <i> It's people who are just</i>
        <i> like you and me</i>


00:05:34.001 --> 00:05:36.735
    <i> who are average citizens</i>


00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:38.804
<i>who are working and doing a job</i>


00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:34.025
      <i> That first breath--</i>


00:06:34.027 --> 00:06:35.927
    <i> it's like a sledgehammer</i>
          <i> to the face.</i>


00:06:36.829 --> 00:06:38.597
    <i> It's your wake up call.</i>


00:06:38.599 --> 00:06:39.831
  <i> It's okay, you're here now.</i>


00:06:39.833 --> 00:06:41.600
     <i> This is the real deal.</i>


00:06:51.712 --> 00:06:53.078
       <i> I couldn't believe</i>
    <i> I was actually doing it.</i>


00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:54.079
      <i> I was actually here.</i>


00:06:54.081 --> 00:06:55.514
     <i> I was in Antarctica--</i>


00:06:55.516 --> 00:06:56.748
   <i> the bottom of the planet.</i>


00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:58.683
 <i> And then my second thought was</i>


00:06:58.685 --> 00:07:01.787
           "Oh crap,
    what have I just done?"


00:07:01.789 --> 00:07:03.054
         Why am I here?


00:07:15.802 --> 00:07:17.569
          <i> I saw Erebus</i>


00:07:17.571 --> 00:07:19.638
        <i> and wanted to go</i>
          <i> hiking there</i>


00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:20.639
     <i> and snowboard down it</i>


00:07:20.641 --> 00:07:22.107
  and I was quickly told "No"


00:07:22.109 --> 00:07:24.075
  and then I was off to work.


00:07:33.219 --> 00:07:35.754
      <i> We had been thinking</i>
  <i> oh we're going to Antarctica</i>


00:07:35.756 --> 00:07:38.623
<i>and when you get here it's like,</i>
    <i> oh yeah I've gotta work</i>


00:07:38.625 --> 00:07:40.926
     <i> And you don't put that</i>
 <i> into your head your first time</i>


00:07:40.928 --> 00:07:43.462
       <i> you're so excited</i>
       <i> about coming down</i>


00:07:43.464 --> 00:07:46.631
  that you forget that you're
 coming down here for a reason.


00:07:49.602 --> 00:07:51.236
 <i> They hurried us off the plane</i>


00:07:51.238 --> 00:07:53.205
      <i> so we couldn't take</i>
         <i> a look around</i>


00:07:53.207 --> 00:07:55.540
        <i> and pretty much</i>
        <i> it was just here</i>


00:07:55.542 --> 00:07:57.642
   <i> and the next thing we knew</i>
        <i> we were in town.</i>


00:08:00.013 --> 00:08:02.981
<i>So the first thing we really saw</i>
   <i> of Antarctica was McMurdo</i>


00:08:02.983 --> 00:08:05.183
   and that's always kind of
    disappointing sometimes.


00:08:20.967 --> 00:08:22.767
      <i> It's a pretty small,</i>
        <i> tight community</i>


00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:25.070
      <i> People pull together</i>
      <i> and get things done.</i>


00:08:25.072 --> 00:08:28.874
 <i> Pretty amazing what gets done</i>
    <i> for what little we have.</i>


00:08:28.876 --> 00:08:31.543
  I've seen a lot of different
 personalities come down here.


00:08:31.545 --> 00:08:34.713
    The ones that seem to do
       the most of it are


00:08:34.715 --> 00:08:37.883
        the die-hards or
   the more grounded people.


00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:47.659
      <i> McMurdo Station sits</i>
 <i> beside the sea on Ross Island.</i>


00:08:47.661 --> 00:08:49.928
   <i> It's dwarfed by Mt. Erebus</i>


00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:53.865
        <i> the southernmost</i>
  <i> active volcano in the world.</i>


00:08:53.867 --> 00:08:57.202
    <i> Three kilometers away on</i>
<i>the other side of the peninsula</i>


00:08:57.204 --> 00:08:59.204
<i>is the small New Zealand outpost</i>


00:08:59.206 --> 00:09:01.239
          <i> Scott Base.</i>


00:09:03.276 --> 00:09:05.911
      <i> Scott Base is a lot</i>
      <i> more typical in size</i>


00:09:05.913 --> 00:09:09.114
       <i> of the other bases</i>
   <i> dotted around Antarctica.</i>


00:09:11.918 --> 00:09:14.819
       <i> And its proximity</i>
     <i> to McMurdo is unusual.</i>


00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:23.995
         <i> There are only</i>
  <i> 30 permanently manned bases</i>


00:09:23.997 --> 00:09:26.865
    <i> on the entire continent</i>
       <i> and most of these</i>


00:09:26.867 --> 00:09:30.001
    <i> have less than 20 people</i>
 <i> living in them in the winter.</i>


00:09:35.741 --> 00:09:39.110
   <i> At Scott Base you have to</i>
 <i> live and work in one building.</i>


00:09:39.112 --> 00:09:42.180
       <i> Our accommodation</i>
      <i> is a very small room</i>


00:09:42.182 --> 00:09:44.249
       <i> It's like a cell,</i>
          <i> if you like,</i>


00:09:44.251 --> 00:09:46.084
         <i> or a cupboard.</i>


00:09:51.958 --> 00:09:56.294
      <i> A lot of things here</i>
 <i> can get to you pretty quickly</i>


00:09:56.296 --> 00:09:57.929
     <i> especially in winter.</i>


00:09:59.966 --> 00:10:01.967
  <i> And you cannot change them.</i>


00:10:03.135 --> 00:10:05.870
     You cannot argue much
       about them either


00:10:05.872 --> 00:10:08.907
      because this person
 will be in front of your eyes,


00:10:08.909 --> 00:10:10.942
  you know, on the next Smoko.


00:10:11.744 --> 00:10:15.246
       <i> So to be tolerant</i>
       <i> is very important.</i>


00:10:22.022 --> 00:10:23.288
     <i> When people ask me why</i>


00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:28.693
   to come back because this
       is the second time


00:10:28.695 --> 00:10:30.028
  I've arrived in Antarctica.


00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:31.730
       <i> For me it's those</i>
        <i> white mountains.</i>


00:10:32.898 --> 00:10:34.899
     <i> Those white mountains</i>


00:10:34.901 --> 00:10:36.234
      <i> and you look across</i>
          <i> and you know</i>


00:10:36.236 --> 00:10:38.637
          <i> just because</i>
      <i> humanity had trouble</i>


00:10:38.639 --> 00:10:41.740
<i>getting here in the first place</i>
 <i> we're relatively new arrivals.</i>


00:10:41.742 --> 00:10:44.909
<i>We have not had the opportunity</i>
  <i> to mess it up very much yet.</i>


00:10:51.351 --> 00:10:54.386
 We have nations getting along
      better in Antarctica


00:10:54.388 --> 00:10:57.389
   than I think they're doing
  anywhere else in the world.


00:10:57.391 --> 00:10:59.391
      It's worth something
      to keep it this way


00:10:59.393 --> 00:11:02.193
  <i> if we can do whatever we can</i>
   <i> to keep the co-operations</i>


00:11:02.195 --> 00:11:03.928
   <i> and just the pure pursuit</i>
           <i> of things</i>


00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:05.697
          <i> that make us</i>
       <i> better as humans.</i>


00:11:06.999 --> 00:11:08.199
   <i> That's happening right now</i>


00:11:08.201 --> 00:11:10.669
       <i> on this continent,</i>
       <i> and it's special.</i>


00:11:17.977 --> 00:11:20.078
 <i> I could live indefinitely here</i>


00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:21.946
    if I could, on occasion,


00:11:23.683 --> 00:11:24.749
            get out.


00:11:24.751 --> 00:11:25.750
       Like I haven't...


00:11:25.752 --> 00:11:27.886
  I've literally gone nowhere


00:11:27.888 --> 00:11:29.254
         since August.


00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:32.290
     <i> Most people that have</i>
      <i> been here that long</i>


00:11:32.292 --> 00:11:34.292
  <i> at least have gone to maybe</i>


00:11:34.294 --> 00:11:35.960
    <i> the Pole or something...</i>


00:11:36.729 --> 00:11:38.263
<i>or at least gotten out of town.</i>


00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:46.938
      <i> You will get to see</i>
         <i> some fun stuff</i>


00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:50.408
   <i> but the main thing you're</i>
   <i> down here for is to work.</i>


00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:52.711
    <i> And I hate to sound cold</i>


00:11:52.713 --> 00:11:53.745
     <i> when it comes to that,</i>


00:11:53.747 --> 00:11:55.447
  <i> but I think a lot of people</i>
        <i> lose that focus</i>


00:11:55.449 --> 00:11:56.948
  and when they come down here


00:11:56.950 --> 00:11:59.050
    and all they get to see
         is a dish room


00:11:59.052 --> 00:12:02.320
       or get to stare at
   a computer the whole time


00:12:02.322 --> 00:12:03.788
     I think they might be
    a little disillusioned.


00:12:14.800 --> 00:12:17.435
        <i> I came down with</i>
    <i> the Italians originally.</i>


00:12:18.237 --> 00:12:20.205
    But having done it once


00:12:20.207 --> 00:12:22.907
   you know I sort of thought
        well that'll do.


00:12:22.909 --> 00:12:23.908
  But a couple of years later


00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:24.809
       I was back again.


00:12:26.445 --> 00:12:27.479
          <i> Still here.</i>


00:12:30.116 --> 00:12:33.485
      <i> Rob has been flying</i>
  <i> in Antarctica for 20 years,</i>


00:12:33.487 --> 00:12:35.320
      <i> but he's never done</i>
         <i> a winter here.</i>


00:12:35.322 --> 00:12:37.756
      <i> Helicopters only fly</i>
         <i> in the summer.</i>


00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:47.132
<i>It's probably safer flying here</i>
      <i> than in New Zealand</i>


00:12:47.134 --> 00:12:50.335
     <i> because you never have</i>
  <i> to contend with the weather.</i>


00:12:50.337 --> 00:12:53.204
    <i> At home there's various</i>
      <i> degrees of weather.</i>


00:12:53.206 --> 00:12:55.140
     Here it's either good
          or it's not.


00:12:55.142 --> 00:12:57.408
         Whereas home,
        it might be okay


00:12:57.410 --> 00:12:58.910
          or just okay


00:12:58.912 --> 00:13:00.311
   or I think I can make it.


00:13:00.313 --> 00:13:01.913
 But there's none of that here.


00:13:10.222 --> 00:13:14.325
       <i> So, what would be</i>
 <i> your favorite place to fly to?</i>


00:13:15.027 --> 00:13:16.361
         Christchurch.


00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:21.466
  No, I like the Dry Valleys.


00:13:21.468 --> 00:13:24.569
    I like the Upper Wright
 and the Beacon Valley as well.


00:13:24.571 --> 00:13:26.805
   It's spectacular scenery,
         it really is.


00:13:33.146 --> 00:13:36.080
       Like Rob, I'm one
       of the lucky ones.


00:13:36.082 --> 00:13:37.982
  <i> The work I do in Antarctica</i>


00:13:37.984 --> 00:13:40.018
      <i> allows me to get out</i>
         <i> and experience</i>


00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:42.453
      <i> some of the grandeur</i>
       <i> and vast emptiness</i>


00:13:42.455 --> 00:13:44.355
   <i> of this amazing continent.</i>


00:14:15.387 --> 00:14:18.256
   <i> Wherever there is research</i>
     <i> happening in the field</i>


00:14:18.258 --> 00:14:20.391
        <i> they need their</i>
    <i> communications support.</i>


00:14:20.393 --> 00:14:22.160
    <i> So I spend a lot of time</i>


00:14:22.162 --> 00:14:23.928
   <i> setting up radio equipment</i>


00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:25.463
      <i> in remote locations.</i>


00:14:37.209 --> 00:14:39.978
     <i> When I first came down</i>
         <i> here in 1998,</i>


00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:44.415
      <i> I was struck by how</i>
   <i> pure and untouched it was.</i>


00:14:44.417 --> 00:14:46.317
   <i> There's still places here</i>


00:14:46.319 --> 00:14:48.987
         <i> where no human</i>
       <i> has ever set foot.</i>


00:15:36.168 --> 00:15:38.937
          <i> I just love</i>
   <i> that there's still places</i>


00:15:38.939 --> 00:15:43.007
    <i> in the world you can go</i>
<i>to experience absolute silence.</i>


00:15:43.275 --> 00:15:45.977
      When you're out here
      and there's no wind


00:15:45.979 --> 00:15:48.613
       your ears are just
 straining to hear something...


00:15:48.615 --> 00:15:50.181
           anything.


00:15:50.183 --> 00:15:51.516
     You just don't realize


00:15:51.518 --> 00:15:53.584
         how much noise
       pollution there is


00:15:53.586 --> 00:15:56.587
       in your daily life
    back in the real world.


00:16:03.329 --> 00:16:06.264
       One very important
      thing when you're...


00:16:06.266 --> 00:16:08.032
working in the deep field is...


00:16:08.034 --> 00:16:11.636
       to make sure never
   to confuse your pee bottle


00:16:11.638 --> 00:16:13.438
    with your water bottle.


00:16:26.652 --> 00:16:28.753
Well...


00:16:28.755 --> 00:16:30.755
      the summit of Erebus


00:16:30.757 --> 00:16:33.391
      and an ice fumarole
         that's growing


00:16:33.393 --> 00:16:36.995
     from steam coming out
   of one of the cracks here.


00:16:41.567 --> 00:16:43.267
           <i> Most days</i>


00:16:43.269 --> 00:16:45.470
     <i> I can't believe I get</i>
     <i> to work in this place.</i>


00:16:51.176 --> 00:16:53.077
<i>But there are days when I think</i>


00:16:53.079 --> 00:16:55.113
 <i> they just can't pay me enough.</i>


00:17:03.222 --> 00:17:06.357
  God, this is an awful place.


00:17:08.227 --> 00:17:11.562
   In theory there should be
   a flag right about there.


00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:16.567
     Ah yeah, there we go.


00:17:20.172 --> 00:17:22.206
        Gotta love GPSs.


00:17:28.747 --> 00:17:30.348
   It's the middle of summer


00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:32.350
    and if we check the time
           it's ah...


00:17:32.352 --> 00:17:34.252
     just after 12 o'clock.


00:17:34.254 --> 00:17:36.287
       That's 12 o'clock
        midnight though.


00:18:48.795 --> 00:18:50.862
        Merry Christmas!


00:19:02.375 --> 00:19:03.641
    Ah, Christmas dinner...


00:19:03.643 --> 00:19:06.477
          making food
  for lots and lots of people.


00:19:06.479 --> 00:19:07.578
        It's a big day.


00:19:12.851 --> 00:19:15.253
 We're spreading holiday cheer.


00:19:15.255 --> 00:19:18.456
       <i> I think for a lot</i>
<i>of younger folks that come down</i>


00:19:18.458 --> 00:19:21.526
        <i> it's a real good</i>
      <i> experience for them.</i>


00:19:21.528 --> 00:19:25.897
   <i> It's probably not a career</i>
        <i> for most people,</i>


00:19:25.899 --> 00:19:28.432
        but for some...
             it is.


00:19:28.434 --> 00:19:31.669
       I think I found it


00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:33.538
    and kind of fell in love
            with it.


00:19:54.193 --> 00:19:56.227
<i>I have been coming to Antarctica</i>


00:19:56.229 --> 00:19:58.563
    <i> for about 13 years now.</i>


00:19:58.565 --> 00:20:00.231
      <i> And when I came down</i>
         <i> the first time</i>


00:20:00.233 --> 00:20:02.900
      <i> I thought, "Ah yeah</i>
<i>this will be great to do once."</i>


00:20:02.902 --> 00:20:04.302
      <i> You know, just come</i>
         <i> to Antarctica.</i>


00:20:04.304 --> 00:20:06.404
      <i> And then I can say,</i>
  <i> "Wow, I went to Antarctica."</i>


00:20:06.406 --> 00:20:08.406
      And then I signed on


00:20:08.408 --> 00:20:10.241
         for a contract
       the following year


00:20:10.243 --> 00:20:11.909
          and then...
  I don't know what happened.


00:20:11.911 --> 00:20:13.411
        I'm still here.


00:20:22.854 --> 00:20:25.856
     There's a saying among
    the women of Antarctica


00:20:25.858 --> 00:20:27.892
      <i> "The odds are good.</i>


00:20:27.894 --> 00:20:29.994
    <i> But the goods, are odd."</i>


00:20:34.633 --> 00:20:36.534
 <i> Christine and I met down here</i>


00:20:36.536 --> 00:20:40.671
     <i> and I proposed to her</i>
     <i> in the winter of 2003.</i>


00:20:40.673 --> 00:20:43.608
         <i> 6 weeks later</i>
  <i> we had an impromptu wedding</i>


00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:45.543
     <i> down here on the ice.</i>


00:20:57.990 --> 00:21:01.559
 <i> The flowers were all hand made</i>
       <i> with folded paper.</i>


00:21:01.561 --> 00:21:02.960
    <i> Christine's friend Lori</i>


00:21:02.962 --> 00:21:05.463
        <i> made up a dress</i>
      <i> from donated fabric.</i>


00:21:05.465 --> 00:21:07.798
      <i> And the plumbers cut</i>
         <i> us some rings</i>


00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:09.567
         <i> made of brass.</i>


00:21:09.569 --> 00:21:12.303
      <i> I borrowed the only</i>
     <i> dinner jacket on base</i>


00:21:12.305 --> 00:21:14.338
           <i> from Eric,</i>
      <i> the station manager</i>


00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:16.540
<i>who then acted as our celebrant.</i>


00:21:19.911 --> 00:21:22.647
       On this night here
        on Ross Island,


00:21:22.649 --> 00:21:24.915
     Christine Marie Gamble


00:21:24.917 --> 00:21:26.817
   and Anthony Brendan Powell


00:21:26.819 --> 00:21:28.452
   are celebrating their love


00:21:28.454 --> 00:21:29.854
         and commitment
         to each other.


00:21:29.856 --> 00:21:31.789
   And tonight they will join


00:21:31.791 --> 00:21:33.524
      as husband and wife


00:21:33.526 --> 00:21:37.061
    in heart, body and soul.


00:21:37.063 --> 00:21:41.799
            Hi, Dad!


00:21:41.801 --> 00:21:43.801
      Just fine. Go ahead.


00:21:49.042 --> 00:21:50.341
           I'm here.


00:21:57.349 --> 00:21:58.482
           Thank you.


00:22:15.568 --> 00:22:18.536
    <i> I've wintered over here</i>
         <i> nine times now</i>


00:22:18.538 --> 00:22:20.905
       <i> and Christine has</i>
      <i> wintered eight times</i>


00:22:20.907 --> 00:22:22.707
       <i> Living on the ice</i>
       <i> is just something</i>


00:22:22.709 --> 00:22:25.576
   <i> that's become so ingrained</i>
         <i> in both of us.</i>


00:24:44.516 --> 00:24:46.750
   <i> The TV version of penguins</i>


00:24:46.752 --> 00:24:48.886
      <i> doesn't prepare you</i>
        <i> for the reality</i>


00:24:48.888 --> 00:24:51.155
      <i> of seeing an actual</i>
        <i> penguin colony.</i>


00:24:53.124 --> 00:24:54.725
      <i> There are thousands</i>


00:24:54.727 --> 00:24:57.094
   <i> of frozen penguin corpses</i>
    <i> that litter the ground.</i>


00:25:04.569 --> 00:25:08.539
 <i> And in summer the sewer stench</i>
     <i> from 100,000 penguins</i>


00:25:08.541 --> 00:25:10.241
   <i> can be pretty unbearable.</i>


00:25:45.977 --> 00:25:49.213
    Ten... nine... eight...


00:25:49.215 --> 00:25:53.050
        Seven... Six...
        Five... Four...


00:25:53.052 --> 00:25:56.153
     Three... Two... One...


00:25:56.155 --> 00:25:58.756
    Happy New Year, McMurdo!


00:26:15.006 --> 00:26:17.207
       <i> I like this place</i>
      <i> for a lot of reasons</i>


00:26:17.209 --> 00:26:19.243
        <i> Part of me feels</i>
     <i> like living at McMurdo</i>


00:26:19.245 --> 00:26:21.245
         <i> is like living</i>
      <i> in Never-never Land.</i>


00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:23.647
    <i> There's a lot of people</i>


00:26:27.252 --> 00:26:28.686
    <i> they have gone in a very</i>


00:26:28.688 --> 00:26:30.054
 <i> different path in their lives.</i>


00:26:30.056 --> 00:26:32.756
<i>And sometimes one of the things</i>
        <i> I like the best</i>


00:26:32.758 --> 00:26:33.757
  <i> is being able to live a life</i>


00:26:33.759 --> 00:26:36.327
      <i> with people who are</i>
        <i> willing to take</i>


00:26:36.329 --> 00:26:37.695
   <i> a slightly different path</i>
    <i> different path in life.</i>


00:26:37.697 --> 00:26:38.996
   And so for the first time
           in my life


00:26:38.998 --> 00:26:41.865
 I'm part of a majority because
   a lot of us feel that way.


00:26:41.867 --> 00:26:44.001
    And I've never been part
    of the majority before.


00:26:52.210 --> 00:26:53.944
        <i> We're in this--</i>
        <i> we're in a box.</i>


00:26:53.946 --> 00:26:55.746
     <i> And there's no escape</i>
        <i> from each other.</i>


00:26:55.748 --> 00:26:56.981
      <i> You can't get away.</i>


00:26:56.983 --> 00:27:00.184
   <i> You're forced to interact</i>
      <i> day in and day out.</i>


00:27:00.186 --> 00:27:02.987
   <i> And you get to know people</i>


00:27:02.989 --> 00:27:05.889
      very, very quickly.


00:27:05.891 --> 00:27:08.926
         Whether those
  relationships be good or bad


00:27:08.928 --> 00:27:11.028
   they're going to be very,
         very intense.


00:27:17.168 --> 00:27:19.236
       That was awesome!


00:27:23.875 --> 00:27:26.043
         <i> Okay, stand by</i>
       <i> for contact again.</i>


00:28:35.447 --> 00:28:37.881
        <i> McMurdo Sound is</i>
          <i> as far south</i>


00:28:37.883 --> 00:28:41.351
   <i> as it is possible to bring</i>
      <i> a ship in the world.</i>


00:28:41.353 --> 00:28:43.353
        <i> And the arrival</i>
       <i> of the supply ship</i>


00:28:43.355 --> 00:28:45.923
      <i> marks the last gasp</i>
        <i> of summer here.</i>


00:29:38.943 --> 00:29:42.446
<i>Once the ship has been offloaded</i>
   <i> it's immediately reloaded</i>


00:29:42.448 --> 00:29:45.415
     <i> with all the rubbish,</i>
    <i> recycling, human waste,</i>


00:29:45.417 --> 00:29:48.418
   <i> and science-related cargo</i>
      <i> from the last year.</i>


00:29:51.489 --> 00:29:53.490
    <i> Nothing is left behind.</i>


00:30:03.868 --> 00:30:06.904
    <i> At the end of February,</i>
   <i> the sun starts to briefly</i>


00:30:06.906 --> 00:30:08.872
  <i> dip back below the horizon.</i>


00:30:14.913 --> 00:30:17.414
        <i> And those of us</i>
   <i> who will be wintering over</i>


00:30:17.416 --> 00:30:19.116
 <i> begin to seek each other out.</i>


00:30:28.126 --> 00:30:32.296
      <i> But most people are</i>
  <i> thinking about heading home.</i>


00:30:51.182 --> 00:30:52.950
   <i> Have you ever been tempted</i>


00:30:52.952 --> 00:30:54.518
 <i> to hang around for the winter?</i>


00:30:54.520 --> 00:30:55.953
              No.


00:31:29.420 --> 00:31:32.155
   <i> When the last plane leaves</i>
           <i> that's it.</i>


00:31:32.157 --> 00:31:34.558
       <i> You're stuck here</i>
     <i> for the next 6 months.</i>


00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:35.993
          <i> No way out.</i>


00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:46.270
      <i> I remember thinking</i>
    <i> what the hell did I do?</i>


00:31:47.205 --> 00:31:49.239
   <i> I should be on that plane.</i>


00:31:49.241 --> 00:31:50.474
              <i> No!</i>


00:32:02.921 --> 00:32:04.187
          <i> God help us!</i>


00:32:08.026 --> 00:32:09.459
        <i> Once it's gone,</i>


00:32:09.461 --> 00:32:11.595
    <i> and the last sound of it</i>
        <i> has disappeared,</i>


00:32:11.597 --> 00:32:14.698
    <i> it's like the whole town</i>
   <i> just breathes a huge sigh.</i>


00:32:16.368 --> 00:32:19.169
       <i> Okay, here we are.</i>


00:32:19.171 --> 00:32:21.405
        <i> You look around</i>
         <i> at the people</i>


00:32:21.407 --> 00:32:24.541
          <i> and you say,</i>
   <i> "Okay, you're my friends--</i>


00:32:24.543 --> 00:32:26.576
        <i> you're my family</i>
    <i> for the next 6 months."</i>


00:32:28.646 --> 00:32:30.580
        <i> My first season</i>
       <i> was in the winter</i>


00:32:30.582 --> 00:32:32.449
    <i> and I do recall watching</i>


00:32:32.451 --> 00:32:34.318
    that last plane leaving
          and thinking


00:32:34.320 --> 00:32:36.954
       boy I hope this is
       what I want to do.


00:33:30.375 --> 00:33:31.608
    <i> Attention all stations.</i>


00:33:31.610 --> 00:33:34.711
     <i> Stand by for a severe</i>
<i>weather condition announcement.</i>


00:33:34.713 --> 00:33:37.614
    <i> McMurdo weather has set</i>
   <i> severe weather condition 2</i>


00:33:37.616 --> 00:33:40.150
  <i> for the following locations</i>


00:33:40.152 --> 00:33:44.187
        <i> McMurdo Station,</i>
    <i> T-Site, Arrival Heights,</i>


00:33:45.323 --> 00:33:48.225
    <i> The Road to Scott Base,</i>


00:33:48.227 --> 00:33:51.428
     <i> Pegasus Field and road</i>
       <i> to Pegasus Field.</i>


00:33:53.164 --> 00:33:56.500
      <i> All other locations</i>
      <i> remain Condition 3.</i>


00:33:56.502 --> 00:33:57.534
    <i> If there's any questions</i>


00:33:57.536 --> 00:33:59.469
 <i> please contact McMurdo Weather</i>


00:33:59.471 --> 00:34:02.472
    <i> at extension 2523-2524.</i>


00:34:02.474 --> 00:34:05.108
        <i> Some people take</i>
   <i> the winters here very well</i>


00:34:05.110 --> 00:34:06.443
       <i> and there's others</i>
         <i> that come down</i>


00:34:06.445 --> 00:34:08.178
    <i> that just don't do well</i>
          <i> with winter.</i>


00:34:08.180 --> 00:34:10.647
  <i> You know there's not enough</i>
       <i> going on for them.</i>


00:34:10.649 --> 00:34:12.349
       <i> There's not enough</i>
     <i> social life for them.</i>


00:34:12.351 --> 00:34:16.053
     It's actually, I think
     a pretty definite line


00:34:16.055 --> 00:34:18.155
     between winter people
     and non-winter people.


00:34:18.157 --> 00:34:20.123
      <i> I mean, it's usually</i>
        <i> fairly obvious.</i>


00:34:30.768 --> 00:34:32.836
      <i> One of my main jobs</i>
       <i> during the winter</i>


00:34:32.838 --> 00:34:37.240
 <i> is to maintain communications</i>
     <i> with the outside world</i>


00:34:37.242 --> 00:34:40.143
    <i> This means regular trips</i>
    <i> out across the ice shelf</i>


00:34:40.145 --> 00:34:43.246
    <i> to the satellite station</i>
        <i> on Black Island.</i>


00:34:44.415 --> 00:34:46.083
       <i> During the summer,</i>


00:34:46.085 --> 00:34:48.752
     <i> it's only a 10-minute</i>
    <i> helicopter flight away.</i>


00:34:48.754 --> 00:34:51.221
     <i> But during the winter</i>
 <i> it means getting in a vehicle,</i>


00:34:51.223 --> 00:34:54.324
      <i> like a Pisten Bully</i>
       <i> and driving there.</i>


00:34:54.326 --> 00:34:57.294
    <i> When conditions are good</i>
  <i> it's about a six-hour drive.</i>


00:34:57.296 --> 00:34:59.796
      <i> But in recent years</i>
 <i> the ice shelf has been having</i>


00:34:59.798 --> 00:35:02.132
        <i> a lot of trouble</i>
       <i> with surface melt.</i>


00:35:04.502 --> 00:35:06.803
          <i> So sometimes</i>
      <i> we've been literally</i>


00:35:06.805 --> 00:35:09.639
  <i> crawling in and out of holes</i>
    <i> taller than the vehicles</i>


00:35:09.641 --> 00:35:11.741
      <i> trying to find a way</i>
         <i> to get there.</i>


00:35:15.780 --> 00:35:17.781
         Hmm, now what?


00:35:23.254 --> 00:35:24.855
        Get around that


00:35:38.836 --> 00:35:41.138
   <i> Hey Bill, can you see any</i>
          <i> way through?</i>


00:35:59.657 --> 00:36:01.391
   Yeah, should be all right.


00:36:11.936 --> 00:36:14.504
        <i> Black Island is</i>
   <i> one of the windiest places</i>


00:36:14.506 --> 00:36:15.639
         <i> on the planet.</i>


00:36:15.641 --> 00:36:18.341
         <i> It's so windy</i>
        <i> that peak winds</i>


00:36:18.343 --> 00:36:19.843
        <i> have never been</i>
      <i> accurately measured</i>


00:36:19.845 --> 00:36:23.346
   <i> because of the wind gauges</i>
      <i> getting blown away.</i>


00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:23.406
       Current wind speed
     is 114 miles per hour


00:37:23.408 --> 00:37:24.975
         or 99 knots--


00:37:24.977 --> 00:37:27.711
  Make that 123 miles an hour.


00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:31.248
      Although the camera
       is on the tripod,


00:37:31.250 --> 00:37:33.683
    you can see the picture
    is actually quite shaky.


00:37:33.685 --> 00:37:34.884
         That's because
       the whole building


00:37:34.886 --> 00:37:36.886
        is being shaken
     by the 100-knot winds


00:37:36.888 --> 00:37:38.855
    we've got at the moment.


00:37:38.857 --> 00:37:42.459
  Just go for a walk out into
  the main satellite dome here


00:37:42.461 --> 00:37:44.594
and show you what it looks like.


00:37:54.705 --> 00:37:57.307
        As you can see,
     it's a wee bit windy.


00:38:28.306 --> 00:38:29.572
       I'll just show you
         what it's like


00:38:29.574 --> 00:38:32.375
     in the bunkhouse here
     after the last storm.


00:38:50.494 --> 00:38:53.330
    All the snow you can see
 in the corner of the room here


00:38:53.332 --> 00:38:54.898
  basically worked its way in


00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:57.467
     through the tiny gaps
      in the window seals.


00:38:58.602 --> 00:39:01.771
     The wind speeds got up
      to 222 miles an hour


00:39:01.773 --> 00:39:04.941
   so this filled up in just
    a matter of a few hours.


00:39:04.943 --> 00:39:07.477
          And there's
    a complete lounge suite


00:39:07.479 --> 00:39:08.545
   in the corner over there.


00:39:08.547 --> 00:39:10.046
     This is the fireplace


00:39:10.048 --> 00:39:14.117
    that's normally used for
     keeping the room warm.


00:40:19.784 --> 00:40:21.050
    <i> In the middle of March,</i>


00:40:21.052 --> 00:40:23.853
      <i> there's that magical</i>
     <i> brief couple of weeks</i>


00:40:23.855 --> 00:40:25.855
     <i> when the sun is rising</i>
          <i> and setting</i>


00:40:25.857 --> 00:40:27.457
        <i> like normal days</i>


00:40:27.459 --> 00:40:29.492
      <i> and you can actually</i>
    <i> leave the curtains open</i>


00:40:29.494 --> 00:40:30.894
    <i> on your window at night</i>


00:40:30.896 --> 00:40:34.631
         <i> to be woken up</i>
 <i> by the sunrise in the morning.</i>


00:41:36.093 --> 00:41:39.863
          <i> We were out</i>
      <i> and the wind came up</i>


00:41:39.865 --> 00:41:42.899
   and there was just moments
      that it was so cold


00:41:42.901 --> 00:41:44.901
         I could really


00:41:44.903 --> 00:41:47.237
 actually feel the danger in it


00:41:47.239 --> 00:41:51.875
        and feel the...
   this crazy like mind thing


00:41:51.877 --> 00:41:53.877
 that happened that was like...


00:41:53.879 --> 00:41:55.845
      it was hard to focus
          on anything


00:41:55.847 --> 00:41:59.883
    except just getting out
  of the conditions we were in


00:41:59.885 --> 00:42:02.652
           and trying
     to just be warm again.


00:42:03.821 --> 00:42:06.723
       <i> It was really hard</i>
       <i> to still focus on</i>


00:42:06.725 --> 00:42:09.225
   <i> gathering all of the gear</i>
           <i> and trying</i>


00:42:09.227 --> 00:42:11.094
  <i> to do what needed to be done</i>


00:42:11.096 --> 00:42:13.096
      <i> rather that just run</i>


00:42:13.098 --> 00:42:14.631
      <i> for whatever vehicle</i>


00:42:14.633 --> 00:42:15.932
    <i> or, you know, it was...</i>


00:42:15.934 --> 00:42:17.767
       it was amazing how


00:42:17.769 --> 00:42:19.736
        your instinct...
           it just...


00:42:19.738 --> 00:42:20.937
         it just snaps.


00:42:23.741 --> 00:42:27.210
      <i> The first time where</i>
  <i> I was just ridiculously cold</i>


00:42:27.212 --> 00:42:29.679
     <i> was actually with you</i>
      <i> just a few days ago</i>


00:42:29.681 --> 00:42:31.881
     <i> taking the shift photo</i>
        <i> up on the hill.</i>


00:42:31.883 --> 00:42:32.916
            <i> Oh yeah.</i>


00:42:34.251 --> 00:42:35.318
     <i> The wind was whipping</i>
         <i> through there.</i>


00:42:35.320 --> 00:42:38.254
    <i> I'd probably say it was</i>
  <i> negative 80 with wind chill.</i>


00:42:38.256 --> 00:42:41.124
         <i> And I remember</i>
<i>looking over at Chris Cavanaugh</i>


00:42:41.126 --> 00:42:43.626
    <i> and tears were streaming</i>
         <i> down his face.</i>


00:42:43.628 --> 00:42:45.628
     <i> He didn't even know it</i>
     <i> because it was so cold</i>


00:42:45.630 --> 00:42:47.564
<i>and the thing that surprised me</i>


00:42:47.566 --> 00:42:49.632
       <i> is when he looked</i>
      <i> at me a second time</i>


00:42:49.634 --> 00:42:52.035
     those tears had frozen
          to his face.


00:42:53.170 --> 00:42:55.872
          <i> So that's...</i>


00:42:55.874 --> 00:42:57.774
 <i> that made an impression on me.</i>


00:42:57.776 --> 00:42:59.742
 <i> Maybe it's time to get inside.</i>


00:43:09.119 --> 00:43:10.954
    We're about 30 K's away


00:43:10.956 --> 00:43:14.290
 from the nearest seawater here
  and it's still quite common


00:43:14.292 --> 00:43:15.925
    to find guys like this.


00:43:15.927 --> 00:43:17.927
     It's a mummified seal


00:43:17.929 --> 00:43:19.062
      that's lost its way


00:43:19.064 --> 00:43:20.663
      and wandered inland.


00:43:20.665 --> 00:43:22.732
       It's an amazingly
       common occurrence.


00:43:43.120 --> 00:43:44.988
<i>It's heartbreaking when you see</i>


00:43:44.990 --> 00:43:47.223
 <i> animals in distress like this</i>


00:43:47.225 --> 00:43:48.992
   <i> because we're not allowed</i>
          <i> to interfere</i>


00:43:52.396 --> 00:43:54.964
   <i> We just have to let nature</i>
        <i> take its course.</i>


00:44:08.012 --> 00:44:09.846
     <i> It can be really hard</i>


00:44:09.848 --> 00:44:12.882
   <i> just being away from home</i>
      <i> and hearing stories.</i>


00:44:12.884 --> 00:44:15.985
 My sister just had a baby boy
          a week ago.


00:44:15.987 --> 00:44:17.387
      And that day was...


00:44:18.689 --> 00:44:19.722
      really bittersweet.


00:44:19.724 --> 00:44:20.890
      It was really cool,


00:44:20.892 --> 00:44:22.358
    but man I wanted nothing
           more than


00:44:22.360 --> 00:44:23.760
  to get the hell out of here


00:44:23.762 --> 00:44:24.794
        just for a day.


00:44:24.796 --> 00:44:26.796
 Go home and enjoy that moment


00:44:26.798 --> 00:44:29.132
         with my family
 because they were all together


00:44:29.134 --> 00:44:31.267
   and then I was down here,


00:44:31.269 --> 00:44:33.870
           you know,
  shooting emails out all day


00:44:33.872 --> 00:44:35.204
     and then on the phone
         a couple times


00:44:35.206 --> 00:44:36.339
and that was really really hard.


00:44:38.075 --> 00:44:39.776
           So for me,


00:44:39.778 --> 00:44:42.445
    what sucks is definitely


00:44:42.447 --> 00:44:44.681
        missing family,
      but more importantly


00:44:44.683 --> 00:44:46.683
   missing these big moments
           with them,


00:44:46.685 --> 00:44:47.684
           you know,


00:44:47.686 --> 00:44:49.318
  my family's lives back home.


00:44:53.390 --> 00:44:55.358
         <i> Well, I mean,</i>
         <i> first of all,</i>


00:44:55.360 --> 00:44:56.959
       <i> I had my concerns.</i>


00:44:56.961 --> 00:44:58.361
     <i> And this is what I do</i>
       <i> always in my life</i>


00:44:58.363 --> 00:44:59.395
  <i> that if I'm ever feeling low</i>


00:44:59.397 --> 00:45:01.431
      <i> I have things around</i>
         <i> just in case.</i>


00:45:01.433 --> 00:45:03.933
 <i> One of the things is I brought</i>
  <i> a lot of photos of my family</i>


00:45:03.935 --> 00:45:05.935
      <i> so I have them taped</i>
         <i> to my fridge.</i>


00:45:05.937 --> 00:45:07.236
      And then I just know


00:45:07.238 --> 00:45:08.471
      that there's always
       people on my side


00:45:08.473 --> 00:45:11.240
even if it seems like the walls
   are closing in down here.


00:45:21.218 --> 00:45:23.419
          <i> It was just</i>
       <i> like any other day</i>


00:45:23.421 --> 00:45:24.287
      <i> and the phone rang.</i>


00:45:26.356 --> 00:45:27.990
     <i> It was my grandmother</i>


00:45:27.992 --> 00:45:30.193
        <i> and immediately</i>
     <i> my heart sunk because</i>


00:45:30.195 --> 00:45:31.961
  <i> I knew something was wrong.</i>


00:45:34.198 --> 00:45:37.400
      <i> And she told me that</i>
   <i> my father had passed away</i>


00:45:37.402 --> 00:45:39.001
             <i> and...</i>


00:45:40.003 --> 00:45:41.204
     <i> it was quite a shock.</i>


00:45:42.239 --> 00:45:44.040
     <i> I never expected that</i>


00:45:44.042 --> 00:45:47.810
           <i> it was...</i>
    <i> it was really difficult.</i>


00:45:47.812 --> 00:45:50.513
            <i> I mean,</i>
     <i> when you lose a parent</i>


00:45:50.515 --> 00:45:54.383
       <i> it's a big thing.</i>
       <i> And to not be able</i>


00:45:54.385 --> 00:45:56.986
     <i> to be with your family</i>
        <i> during that time</i>


00:45:56.988 --> 00:46:00.490
       <i> and not being able</i>
     <i> to go to the funeral.</i>


00:46:00.492 --> 00:46:04.360
           <i> I just...</i>
  <i> it leaves things unfinished.</i>


00:46:10.100 --> 00:46:13.102
      <i> I wanted to be there</i>
         <i> for my family.</i>


00:46:13.104 --> 00:46:14.804
      <i> I needed to be there</i>


00:46:14.806 --> 00:46:15.805
        <i> and I couldn't.</i>


00:46:32.389 --> 00:46:35.057
     <i> It's probably the most</i>
  <i> isolated place on the planet</i>


00:46:35.059 --> 00:46:37.326
     and that's really hard
         to adjust to.


00:46:37.328 --> 00:46:39.562
    I can see people who...


00:46:39.564 --> 00:46:42.265
      one winter would be
        enough for them.


00:46:45.135 --> 00:46:46.903
   <i> It's really hard for a lot</i>
           <i> of people.</i>


00:46:59.983 --> 00:47:01.284
       <i> I found that there</i>
         <i> was this curve</i>


00:47:01.286 --> 00:47:03.152
      <i> where I just really</i>
      <i> started missing like</i>


00:47:03.154 --> 00:47:04.554
  <i> eating sushi and Indian food</i>


00:47:04.556 --> 00:47:06.022
      <i> and going to movies</i>
        <i> and just like...</i>


00:47:06.024 --> 00:47:07.089
           the life.


00:47:07.091 --> 00:47:08.324
And then it became like so much


00:47:08.326 --> 00:47:09.992
       that I just really
        missed it a lot


00:47:09.994 --> 00:47:12.962
        and then it just
       dropped off and...


00:47:12.964 --> 00:47:14.063
        I don't miss...


00:47:14.065 --> 00:47:15.464
   I don't know if it's like


00:47:15.466 --> 00:47:17.200
    a post-traumatic stress
       disorder response


00:47:17.202 --> 00:47:18.568
    or I don't want to think
       about what I miss


00:47:18.570 --> 00:47:20.036
 or I'll get really distressed
         or what it is,


00:47:20.038 --> 00:47:22.405
          but there's
     not a lot that I miss.


00:47:22.407 --> 00:47:24.974
       I've been thinking
     about avocados lately.


00:47:24.976 --> 00:47:26.909
   An avocado would be nice.


00:47:26.911 --> 00:47:28.144
      You may want to have


00:47:29.213 --> 00:47:31.013
      a toast with avocado


00:47:31.015 --> 00:47:33.549
     and then you can only
        dream about it.


00:47:33.551 --> 00:47:35.117
        But simple as...


00:47:37.120 --> 00:47:39.589
   black tea with fresh milk


00:47:39.591 --> 00:47:41.157
     or perhaps even better


00:47:41.159 --> 00:47:42.925
       with fresh cream.


00:47:42.927 --> 00:47:44.026
          <i> Dark bread.</i>


00:47:45.529 --> 00:47:46.495
          <i> Cauliflower.</i>


00:47:47.364 --> 00:47:49.031
        <i> It's brilliant.</i>


00:47:49.033 --> 00:47:52.001
          <i> Just to have</i>
  <i> a fresh head of cauliflower</i>


00:47:52.003 --> 00:47:53.169
    <i> would be very nice now.</i>


00:47:58.942 --> 00:48:00.309
            Noooooo!


00:48:01.946 --> 00:48:04.247
    It's no good we can't do
      anything for him now


00:48:04.249 --> 00:48:06.115
     <i> In the winter of 2008,</i>


00:48:06.117 --> 00:48:07.617
      <i> I started a 48-hour</i>


00:48:07.619 --> 00:48:09.218
    <i> Antarctic film festival.</i>


00:48:13.357 --> 00:48:17.126
   <i> and invited all the bases</i>
<i>on the continent to participate.</i>


00:48:22.099 --> 00:48:23.466
 <i> One of the greatest pleasures</i>


00:48:23.468 --> 00:48:25.334
    <i> of watching these films</i>
           <i> each year</i>


00:48:25.336 --> 00:48:29.005
  <i> is just getting to see what</i>
  <i> the other bases look like...</i>


00:48:32.309 --> 00:48:33.576
   <i> and who else is out there</i>


00:48:33.578 --> 00:48:35.478
         <i> wintering over</i>
          <i> on the ice.</i>


00:48:52.162 --> 00:48:53.529
           <i> Carolina,</i>


00:48:53.531 --> 00:48:54.630
   <i> great to hear your voice.</i>


00:48:54.632 --> 00:48:56.132
        Yes how are you?


00:48:57.434 --> 00:48:59.468
I'm good. Yeah, yeah, I'm fine.


00:48:59.470 --> 00:49:00.636
    Everything's good here.


00:49:00.638 --> 00:49:02.305
           Just um...


00:49:02.307 --> 00:49:03.105
           having...


00:49:03.107 --> 00:49:05.341
      having my breakfast
        and some coffee.


00:49:08.111 --> 00:49:10.079
     <i> How are your parents?</i>


00:49:32.502 --> 00:49:34.370
        -<i> Another hour?</i>
            -<i> Ummm.</i>


00:49:38.709 --> 00:49:40.743
       <i> Are we ever going</i>
     <i> to see the sun again?</i>


00:49:41.578 --> 00:49:42.712
            One day.


00:50:10.574 --> 00:50:12.241
  <i> Guess you start to get used</i>


00:50:12.243 --> 00:50:14.010
      <i> to living in a world</i>
         <i> without light.</i>


00:50:17.247 --> 00:50:20.116
       <i> The days get short</i>
    <i> and the nights get long</i>


00:50:20.118 --> 00:50:22.251
<i>'till the nights get real long.</i>


00:50:22.253 --> 00:50:24.086
        <i> Towards the end</i>
     <i> of the month there...</i>


00:50:25.088 --> 00:50:26.455
      there's no more sun


00:50:26.457 --> 00:50:29.725
             and...
     that's all she wrote.


00:50:55.719 --> 00:50:57.753
          <i> I was scared</i>
     <i> of the coming darkness</i>


00:50:57.755 --> 00:50:59.121
      <i> the coming darkness</i>


00:50:59.123 --> 00:51:02.158
       <i> for so many months</i>
         <i> and I thought</i>


00:51:02.160 --> 00:51:03.392
  is that going to get to me?


00:51:03.394 --> 00:51:05.061
  Is that going to make me...


00:51:05.063 --> 00:51:06.162
 well, how will that affect me?


00:51:06.164 --> 00:51:08.531
  I was pretty scared of that
     and how that might...


00:51:08.533 --> 00:51:11.200
        <i> just change me.</i>


00:51:13.103 --> 00:51:14.670
       <i> I would correspond</i>
       <i> with winter-overs</i>


00:51:14.672 --> 00:51:16.272
     <i> when I was off the ice</i>


00:51:16.274 --> 00:51:17.606
       <i> and you could just</i>
    <i> see it in their emails.</i>


00:51:18.675 --> 00:51:19.542
       They would change.


00:51:19.544 --> 00:51:22.111
        And I'm like wow
  is that going to change me?


00:51:22.113 --> 00:51:23.179
         I don't know.


00:51:28.618 --> 00:51:31.287
   <i> As it is the light changes</i>
       <i> enough in the sky</i>


00:51:31.289 --> 00:51:32.655
  <i> that it's still seems to me</i>


00:51:32.657 --> 00:51:35.257
      <i> that the sun is just</i>
       <i> around the corner.</i>


00:51:35.259 --> 00:51:37.726
 <i> And I think that's had a great</i>
      <i> effect on me because</i>


00:51:37.728 --> 00:51:38.594
  <i> it just reminds me that no,</i>


00:51:38.596 --> 00:51:39.829
      <i> it's not just dark.</i>


00:51:40.730 --> 00:51:41.597
     <i> Attention all stations</i>


00:51:41.599 --> 00:51:44.400
      <i> standby for a severe</i>
<i>weather condition announcement.</i>


00:51:44.402 --> 00:51:47.236
    <i> McMurdo weather has set</i>
  <i> Severe Weather Condition One</i>


00:51:49.740 --> 00:51:52.775
    <i> McMurdo Station, T-site,</i>
        <i> Arrival Heights,</i>


00:51:52.777 --> 00:51:54.310
         <i> Pegasus Field,</i>


00:51:54.312 --> 00:51:56.112
        <i> Williams Field,</i>
   <i> road to Williams Field...</i>


00:51:57.547 --> 00:51:58.747
        Check this out.


00:52:01.551 --> 00:52:05.654
      This is the entryway
          to our dorm.


00:52:05.656 --> 00:52:08.190
    Normally this has carpet
         on the ground


00:52:08.192 --> 00:52:10.459
     and the walls are just
   normally walls, you know,


00:52:10.461 --> 00:52:11.727
             But...


00:52:11.729 --> 00:52:13.329
      We're going to open
       the door right now


00:52:13.331 --> 00:52:14.497
     and it's a little bit
        like opening up


00:52:14.499 --> 00:52:15.664
   the door to another world.


00:52:17.267 --> 00:52:18.834
         Are you ready?


00:52:28.879 --> 00:52:30.846
    It's a little bit blowy
      out there right now.


00:52:49.533 --> 00:52:51.800
           <i> You know,</i>
 <i> when you watch television here</i>


00:52:51.802 --> 00:52:52.935
   <i> and you see all the things</i>


00:52:52.937 --> 00:52:54.770
      <i> that are going wrong</i>
          <i> in the world</i>


00:52:54.772 --> 00:52:56.205
       <i> you know that's...</i>


00:52:56.207 --> 00:52:57.373
        <i> it's like thank</i>
       <i> goodness I'm here.</i>


00:52:57.375 --> 00:52:59.675
       <i> But then you also</i>
     <i> then watch shows where</i>


00:52:59.677 --> 00:53:02.378
   <i> you see swaying palm trees</i>


00:53:03.513 --> 00:53:05.648
      <i> and people laughing</i>


00:53:05.650 --> 00:53:07.383
       and bucolic scenes


00:53:07.385 --> 00:53:08.384
     and you're not there.


00:53:08.386 --> 00:53:10.419
     You're here where it's
    dark and cold and windy.


00:54:07.377 --> 00:54:08.911
   <i> I was out on the sea ice,</i>


00:54:08.913 --> 00:54:11.013
          <i> and suddenly</i>
    <i> off of Observation Hill</i>


00:54:11.015 --> 00:54:13.949
         <i> comes rolling</i>
      <i> these just waves...</i>


00:54:13.951 --> 00:54:16.018
       <i> waves of green...</i>


00:54:16.020 --> 00:54:18.921
        <i> like fairy dust.</i>


00:54:18.923 --> 00:54:20.389
      Like a giant curtain
         of fairy dust


00:54:20.391 --> 00:54:22.391
    just kind of undulating
            over me


00:54:22.393 --> 00:54:25.661
   that filled the whole sky
and just moved in waves across.


00:54:25.663 --> 00:54:28.664
     And I thought this is
   either what it looks like


00:54:28.666 --> 00:54:29.965
      if aliens are about
         to abduct you.


00:54:29.967 --> 00:54:35.271
 and almost like you feel like
you could reach up and touch it.


00:54:35.273 --> 00:54:37.506
     Or if you're a person
   who believes in Heaven...


00:54:37.508 --> 00:54:38.974
   maybe this is what you see
     when you go to Heaven.


00:54:38.976 --> 00:54:40.309
         I'm not sure,


00:54:40.311 --> 00:54:42.978
 but it was really an emotional
life-changing experience for me.


00:54:42.980 --> 00:54:47.016
  I mean I really found myself
   not realizing I'd done it.


00:54:47.018 --> 00:54:49.718
       When I figured out
   where my body position was


00:54:49.720 --> 00:54:52.588
   I was actually on my knees
       and I was crying.


00:54:52.590 --> 00:54:53.922
   Like that's how beautiful
         it was to me.


00:56:24.581 --> 00:56:25.714
     It's the month of June


00:56:25.716 --> 00:56:28.417
         and right now
  it's the middle of the day.


00:56:55.445 --> 00:56:57.980
           I grew up
      in a very rural area


00:56:57.982 --> 00:56:59.882
         so I could see
      the stars very well.


00:56:59.884 --> 00:57:01.150
     ...In rural Minnesota.


00:57:01.152 --> 00:57:02.684
 And I've been in the mountains


00:57:02.686 --> 00:57:03.719
    and, you know, Colorado.


00:57:03.721 --> 00:57:05.888
         And I thought
  wow the sky looks beautiful.


00:57:05.890 --> 00:57:08.023
    But I never knew a star
      could flash like...


00:57:08.025 --> 00:57:10.025
       fluorescent pink..
      fluorescent green...


00:57:10.027 --> 00:57:10.926
      fluorescent blue...
            white...


00:57:10.928 --> 00:57:12.127
     just in that rotation.


00:57:12.129 --> 00:57:13.429
       just blink-blink-
       blink-blink-blink.


00:57:13.431 --> 00:57:14.963
        One star like...


00:57:14.965 --> 00:57:16.131
     it looks like a strobe
          light to me.


00:57:16.133 --> 00:57:19.034
     There's a lot of them
           that look


00:57:19.036 --> 00:57:20.502
      like a strobe light
           up there.


00:57:20.504 --> 00:57:21.537
         And you would
         never know...


00:57:21.539 --> 00:57:22.838
      you'd never know...


00:57:22.840 --> 00:57:24.440
    what the sky looks like.


00:57:25.175 --> 00:57:26.842
        It's wonderful.


00:58:02.846 --> 00:58:06.482
      <i> I loved the 24 hours</i>
          <i> of darkness.</i>


00:58:06.484 --> 00:58:08.851
   <i> Maybe on full moon nights,</i>
       <i> and other nights,</i>


00:58:08.853 --> 00:58:09.585
   <i> I would see the mountains.</i>


00:58:09.587 --> 00:58:11.920
    <i> But sometimes I'd forget</i>
 <i> the mountains were even there.</i>


00:58:11.922 --> 00:58:15.157
     <i> And so my entire world</i>
 <i> was just in the little spaces</i>


00:58:15.159 --> 00:58:16.725
      <i> and walking around.</i>


00:58:22.499 --> 00:58:24.933
         <i> It really does</i>
    <i> change your perception.</i>


00:58:49.893 --> 00:58:51.193
   <i> Towards the end of summer,</i>


00:58:51.195 --> 00:58:53.061
      <i> the thing I was most</i>
     <i> looking forward to was</i>


00:58:53.063 --> 00:58:54.763
     <i> seeing darkness again.</i>


00:58:54.765 --> 00:58:56.832
       <i> Like I just wanted</i>
     <i> to see some nighttime.</i>


00:58:59.836 --> 00:59:01.003
 <i> And now that it's night time.</i>


00:59:02.672 --> 00:59:05.908
  <i> I don't really miss the day</i>
      <i> that much right now.</i>


00:59:11.214 --> 00:59:13.215
     <i> We have three dinners</i>
        <i> here each year.</i>


00:59:13.217 --> 00:59:15.617
      <i> Of course our sunset</i>
     <i> now at the midwinter,</i>


00:59:15.619 --> 00:59:18.887
       and as we all know
 that sun rise isn't far away.


00:59:20.557 --> 00:59:22.591
        So I'd like you
     to charge your glasses


00:59:22.593 --> 00:59:25.127
       and to friendships
 and long-lasting friendships.


00:59:25.129 --> 00:59:26.028
           Thank you.


00:59:26.030 --> 00:59:27.296
            Cheers!


00:59:30.567 --> 00:59:32.100
            Cheers!


00:59:36.205 --> 00:59:37.873
  It's very cool that there is


00:59:37.875 --> 00:59:39.875
 a unique holiday in Antarctica


00:59:39.877 --> 00:59:42.844
      that people from all
    over the world celebrate


00:59:42.846 --> 00:59:44.212
     and we send greetings
         to each other,


00:59:44.214 --> 00:59:45.180
  and good will to each other,


00:59:45.182 --> 00:59:47.249
    and remember the history
      that brought us here


00:59:47.251 --> 00:59:48.684
       in the first place


00:59:48.686 --> 00:59:50.285
         and the goals
      of what we're up to


00:59:50.287 --> 00:59:51.253
    here in the first place.


00:59:59.663 --> 01:00:01.964
        And it's just...


01:00:01.966 --> 01:00:03.332
        it's wonderful.


01:00:03.334 --> 01:00:05.334
         I really liked
      the mid-winter time.


01:00:32.028 --> 01:00:33.929
            Oh God.


01:00:39.970 --> 01:00:41.403
     Well, that woke me up.


01:00:43.006 --> 01:00:43.905
        Come on, Brody.


01:00:43.907 --> 01:00:44.840
         Do it. Do it.
             Do it.


01:00:55.318 --> 01:00:56.051
           How is it?


01:00:56.053 --> 01:00:57.219
             Cold.


01:01:00.857 --> 01:01:03.058
         <i> The other day</i>
     <i> I came in from outside</i>


01:01:03.060 --> 01:01:06.028
     <i> and Christine asked me</i>
   <i> is it cold outside today?</i>


01:01:06.030 --> 01:01:06.895
           <i> And I said</i>
            <i> "Oh no.</i>


01:01:06.897 --> 01:01:08.030
       <i> It's not too bad."</i>


01:01:08.032 --> 01:01:10.232
    <i> And then it struck me...</i>


01:01:10.234 --> 01:01:11.266
         <i> the absurdity</i>
     <i> of the conversation</i>


01:01:11.268 --> 01:01:13.001
  because we're in Antarctica,


01:01:13.003 --> 01:01:14.403
   It's the middle of winter,


01:01:14.405 --> 01:01:17.005
      and she's asking me
     if it's cold outside.


01:01:17.007 --> 01:01:19.408
          And secondly
        it was 40 below.


01:01:19.410 --> 01:01:21.143
        We haven't seen
       the sun for months


01:01:21.145 --> 01:01:22.878
and I thought it was a nice day.


01:01:22.880 --> 01:01:26.948
      So it's fascinating
    the way our perceptions


01:01:26.950 --> 01:01:30.185
   of what we consider normal
         have shifted.


01:01:31.087 --> 01:01:32.954
        <i> Nine, zero, six.</i>
           <i> Firehouse</i>


01:01:34.757 --> 01:01:36.291
    <i> Go for nine, zero, six.</i>


01:01:36.293 --> 01:01:38.093
       Hey just checking
        on the progress


01:01:38.095 --> 01:01:39.861
 is Scott Base road open again?


